.Dd October 3, 2026
.Dt MEMORY 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm memory
.Nd memory allocation and accounting
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The kernel accounts for physical memory from the memory map the loader
passes, Multiboot's or multiboot2's, or under UEFI the firmware's EFI
memory map, using 64-bit arithmetic.
It manages the usable memory below its reach: 4 GiB on i686, whose
physical addresses are 32 bits, and on x86-64 all of the memory, up to the
64 TiB its direct map covers.
On i686 its virtual address space occupies the gigabyte starting at
.Li 0xC0000000 ,
with 256 page tables shared by every page directory; on x86-64 it is the
upper half of the address space, where all of physical memory is mapped
from
.Li 0xFFFF800000000000 .
.Pp
The first 32 MiB of physical memory is the DMA pool, below 4 GiB on both,
and on i686 it is identity-mapped: virtual and physical addresses there
are equal.
The frame allocators used for user pages search above it first.
They allocate below it only while more than sixty-four frames remain free
there.
.Ss Process address spaces
Virtual memory areas (VMAs) describe the program, the heap and mappings.
Brk grows or shrinks one heap range.
Growth reserves addresses only; pages are allocated on first access.
Mmap2 creates mappings and munmap removes them.
.Pp
A fault on the page just below the stack region, at or above the limit
set when the stack was created, grows the stack.
Any other unresolved user-mode page fault sends the thread
.Dv SIGSEGV ,
which a handler may catch; a legal access for which no frame can be
allocated ends the process with
.Dv SIGKILL ,
as Linux's OOM killer ends one, there being no swap.
.Dv RLIMIT_AS
bounds the bytes covered by the regions; see
.Xr limits 7 .
A process may have at most
.Dv VMA_LIMIT
regions.
Mapping exhaustion is reported once per process in the form
.Dl vm: OPERATION: pid PID (NAME) has used all N of its mappings
.Ss Copy-on-write fork
Fork shares the parent's pages read-only.
The first write to a shared writable page copies it for the writer.
.Ss Page cache and reserve
The page cache provides a window of 4096-byte pages over a disk root.
Pages are filled on demand and evicted under memory pressure.
.Pp
A frame reserve is kept for root's login so that a machine short of memory
can still be entered.
The TCP listener drops an incoming SYN when the free-frame count is at
or below the reserve.
.Ss Kernel heap
The kernel heap supplies process records, sockets, requests and buffers.
Its region is 256 MiB on i686.
On x86-64 it is sized at boot to an eighth of the memory, 256 MiB at the
least and 512 GiB at the most.
The buffer cache holds at most half of it.
.Pa /proc/meminfo
reports memory totals.
.Pp
Each process has a kernel stack of 16 KiB with an unmapped page below it,
which Linux on i386 does not have.
A process that runs its kernel stack past the base faults on that page,
and the kernel stops with a record naming the process; see
.Sx DIAGNOSTICS .
.Pp
In the source tree,
.Ql tests/run memory
checks memory accounting.
.Ql tests/run pools
opens and closes a pipe, a pty and a socket a thousand times each and
checks that the heap bytes in use return to their initial value and that
the heap page count differs from its initial count by at most two.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /proc/meminfo
.It Pa /proc/meminfo
Memory accounting totals.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
At boot the kernel reports:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
memory: usable N KiB, unreachable N KiB, reserved N KiB, managed N KiB
.Ed
.Pp
Usable is the available memory below the reach and unreachable the
available memory at or above it, so on x86-64 unreachable is 0 on any
machine of less than 64 TiB; reserved is what the boot took of the usable
memory, and managed what the frame allocator offers.
.Pp
A kernel stack that runs out stops the machine with a record that says
.Dl pid N ran past the end of its kernel stack
and the panic
.Dl panic: stopped: double fault: a kernel stack ran out
or, when the page fault's own frame fits on the stack,
.Ql page fault: a kernel stack ran out .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr brk 2 ,
.Xr mmap2 2 ,
.Xr proc 5 ,
.Xr limits 7
